> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Finding.** The infographic prints {{n_total}} numbers. Under one verdict rule fixed in code, {{n_reproduz}} reproduce exactly from public data, {{n_aproxima}} approximate, {{n_nao}} do not reproduce and {{n_nv}} have no official series to check. It also leaves out {{omitido_pct}}% of the months between 1964 and 2026 ({{omitido_meses}} of {{total_meses}}): five governments.
> This repository does not rank governments and does not say whom the omissions favour. Intent cannot be measured from data.

> [!NOTE]
> Hebrew is a machine translation with no human review of right-to-left rendering.

## What this contributes

1. **A verdict rule fixed in code** (`code/govfc/verdict.py`) applied to every number of the infographic, with the primary specification declared per indicator and every alternative tried reported as sensitivity.
2. **Data with a hash chain from the moment of download.** Every file is recorded in an append-only `manifest.jsonl` where each line carries the hash of the previous one; the whole repository is then sealed with one SHA-256 `chain_hash`.
3. **A catalogue of method and rule breaks** ({{n_quebras}} entries) with the reason each source gives, the effect measured where two methods overlap, and which infographic cells cross each break; plus a curated layer of {{n_obs}} observations in {{n_ind}} indicators, with the source edition (vintage) on every row.

